Q: Using Biopython and accessing the NCBI databases, how should my search term variable look like? A: <p>Please try to substitute your string</p>
<pre><code>search_term = f&quot;{species_name} AND {&quot;reference genome&quot;}&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>with</p>
<pre><code>search_term = f&quot;{species_name} AND {'reference genome'}&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>and comment wor a while the second definition of <code>search_term</code></p>
<pre><code>    search_term = (
        f&quot;{species_name}[ORGN]AND{&quot;Annotated by GenBank submitter&quot;}&quot;
        # AND &quot;
        # f&quot;chromosome[Assembly Level] OR complete genome[Assembly Level]&quot;
    )
</code></pre>
<p>By doing this you can get something like</p>
<pre><code>GCA_040285355.1
</code></pre>

Q: CSS override rules and specificity A: <h2>How to use !important:</h2>
<p>This is an old post, but the answers don't mention <strong>why</strong> <code>!important</code> failed to work.  Your code has an error.  When using <code>!important</code>, it must be placed after the value, before the semicolon.</p>
<p><strong>Working code:</strong></p>
<pre><code>td.rule2 div {
    background-color: #ffff00 !important;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can read more about <code>!important</code> here:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/important" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/important</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Also be aware that <code>!important</code> is almost always overkill.  I've only used it a tiny handful of times in over 25 years of writing HTML and CSS.</p>
<h2>How to get more specific:</h2>
<p>You can just add any parent item when needing to be more specific than other rules, even ones like <code>html</code> or <code>body</code>.  In your example of a table cell, you can use the parent <code>table</code>, <code>tr</code>, or <code>td</code> beforehand to add specificity.</p>
<p><strong>For example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>table tr td.rule2 {
    background-color: #ffff00;
}
</code></pre>
